B08d strtold shared-failure source reduction, 2026-09-27
====================================================

Source: musl-hex-tail-reduction.c. This is a positive-normal-input reduction of
musl 1.2.6 src/internal/floatscan.c:hexfloat, not a replacement parser or a
patch to the pinned benchmark. It retains digit collection, sticky-tail update,
normalization and the final rounded x+y sum; the final exact power-of-two scale
does not require a library call.

For binary128, 113/4+1 is 29. With a leading hexadecimal 1, 29 significant
digits already occupy the full 113-bit significand. The source's later
`y += 0.5 * scale` turns every nonzero remaining tail into a halfway case:
  input ...111281: remaining 0x81/0x100 is above halfway, not exactly halfway;
  input ...11111: remaining 0x1/0x10 is below halfway, not exactly halfway.
Both artificial ties round to even, producing ...1112. Exact integer
quotient/remainder rounding instead gives ...1113 and ...1111, respectively.
Keeping one additional hexadecimal guard digit is a diagnostic control that
distinguishes these two inputs correctly. It is not claimed as a complete
parser repair across other signs, formats, directed modes or subnormals.

At O0 and O2:
  C-HIR interpreter: 2/2 PASS.
  EvoIR interpreter: 2/2 PASS.
  GCC14 native: 2/2 PASS.
  Clang19 native: 2/2 PASS.
  EvoCC native: 2/2 PASS.
Each execution asserts all four results: both original incorrect values and
both correct guard-digit controls. These are passes of the diagnostic model,
not passes of the original strtold library tests.

